+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Shipped              |
+
+Tool definitions already expose the JSON Schema sent to providers. The registry is the enforcement boundary for that same schema, so malformed model-generated calls are handled consistently before consumer code sees them.
+
+## Registry behavior
+
+- `ToolRegistry.execute` rejects input that violates `required`, declared types, or `enum` before the executor runs by returning a `ToolResponse` that identifies the property path and failed constraint without including property values; schema-valid input, including unknown properties, reaches the executor unchanged exactly once, while an unknown tool name still throws. ([validated by: validates calls before execution without changing valid input or disclosing values](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L34))
+- Registering a static definition compiles its schema immediately, rejects an invalid schema with the tool name, and reuses that compiled schema across executions. ([validated by: compiles and checks a static schema at registration only](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L75))
+- A function-valued definition is resolved for each call and the schema current for that call is used for validation, with compiled validators cached by resolved schema object rather than becoming stale. ([validated by: validates each dynamic definition against its current schema](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L114))
+
+## Conversation behavior
+
+- A rejected tool call is returned to the provider as a tool result rather than thrown out of the conversation, allowing a corrected call on a later bounded tool round to complete `processMessage`. ([validated by: returns a schema rejection to the model so it can correct the call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L148))
+
+## Contract notes
+
+The registry applies the declared schema as written: it does not add `additionalProperties: false`, coerce values, apply defaults, or provide an opt-out. The validator is a runtime dependency, and `execute` points to this specification with a one-line documentation comment. Executor authors can rely on inputs having passed their declared schema while still accounting for unknown keys.
